\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 behavioural risks to monitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1. Becoming overly instrumental with people\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Observable behavior:
\f0\b0  selectively sharing information, framing colleagues mainly in terms of usefulness, using pressure or leverage before trying influence or dialogue, or ending conversations once the desired outcome seems clear.\

\f1\b Early warning sign:
\f0\b0  "I don't need them to agree; I just need them to do it."\

\f1\b Countermeasure:
\f0\b0  Before acting, ask, 
\f2\i "Would I consider this process fair if I were on the other side of the decision?"
\f0\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Underestimating interpersonal impact because you feel calm\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Observable behavior:
\f0\b0  responding to distress with facts and solutions before acknowledging the person's experience; appearing dismissive when you believe the emotional reaction is disproportionate; becoming unusually blunt under time pressure.\

\f1\b Early warning sign:
\f0\b0  "They're reacting emotionally; the facts are straightforward."\

\f1\b Countermeasure:
\f0\b0  acknowledge impact before moving to analysis or solutions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 3. Overconfidence in your reading of motives\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Observable behavior:
\f0\b0  assuming you know why someone is resisting, questioning a decision, delaying, or disagreeing; testing loyalty; forming conclusions based on patterns without checking the person's explanation.\

\f1\b Early warning sign:
\f0\b0  a confident internal story about someone's intentions that you haven't actually verified.\

\f1\b Countermeasure:
\f0\b0  distinguish 
\f1\b observation, interpretation, and evidence
\f0\b0  before acting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Top 3 strengths/protective capacities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1. Humility as an active control mechanism\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 A 
\f1\b 75 humility
\f0\b0  score is potentially your strongest safeguard. Used deliberately, humility can interrupt the "I see the game clearly, therefore I must be right" pattern.\
Build on it by making dissent routine, explicitly acknowledging uncertainty, and publicly changing your mind when evidence changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Empathy without sacrificing decisiveness\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 An 
\f1\b empathy score of 61
\f0\b0  suggests you may have a meaningful capacity to understand others while still being able to act. The goal isn't to become softer; it's to become 
\f1\b more accurate about impact
\f0\b0 .\
Use empathy as an information-gathering tool: 
\f2\i What is this person experiencing? What have I missed? What will this decision signal to the team?
\f0\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 3. Ethical orientation as a decision gate\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Your 
\f1\b 68 ethical-leadership
\f0\b0  score can become a practical brake on strategic impulses.\
Rather than asking only, 
\f2\i "Will this work?"
\f0\i0 , add:\
